// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  THE REDUCTION IS LOAD-BEARING — offer the desk Contract C exactly as it ARRIVES, and the
//  desk refuses it.
//
//  Run me with the RhoCalc interpreter:
//    target/debug/rholang demos/flt-assay-desk/desk-refuses-the-unreduced-arrival.rho
//
//  WHAT CHANGED FROM `desk-accepts-constant.rho`
//  ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Nothing except the datum. The channel is the same, the receive pattern is the same, and the
//  `where` clause is character-for-character the same — it still asks for the constant
//  combinator K. What rests on @"assay" is Contract C in the form it ARRIVES in:
//
//        offered here      lambda`(lam x. x, lam a. lam b. a)`      = the APPLICATION (I K)
//        offered in Beat 3 lambda`lam a. lam b. a`                  = its NORMAL FORM K
//
//  and those are different terms. `(I K)` β-reduces to `K` — but only if something reduces it.
//
//  WHY THIS BEAT EXISTS
//  ────────────────────
//  It is the difference between a demo in which the reduction is decorative and one in which it
//  is load-bearing. The desk's predicate is over the RESULT of the foreign computation, so the
//  run-to-completion in `contract-c.rho` is not a warm-up act: it is the step that turns an
//  un-acceptable arrival into an acceptable result. Take the reduction away and the same desk,
//  with the same guard, settles nothing.
//
//  It also rules out a second way the demo could pass for the wrong reason: a guard that
//  compared SOURCE TEXT, or that matched loosely on the guest-term envelope rather than on the
//  term, would accept this file's datum too. It does not.
//
//  EXPECT
//  ──────
//        @"OUT": (the program rested without publishing any observation)
//        the un-reduced arrival still resting, untouched, on @"assay"
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

@"assay"!(lambda`(lam x. x, lam a. lam b. a)`) |

for(@lambda`${r}` <- @"assay" where lambda`${r}` == lambda`lam a. lam b. a`) {
  @"OUT"!(lambda`${r}`)
}
